# STEP 4 — DELIVERY AUTHENTICATION ISOLATION

This is one of the most important parts of Phase 3.

The new application must use the same Firebase project but must have its own Firebase app/auth initialization boundary.

Create a Delivery-specific Firebase service, for example:

apps/delivery/src/services/firebase.ts

Use the existing public Firebase web configuration and the same existing Firebase project.

Use a uniquely named Firebase app instance, such as:

`initializeApp(firebaseConfig, 'ScaleKitchenDelivery')`

Initialize the Delivery app's own:

- Firebase Auth instance.
- Firestore instance.
- Firebase Functions instance.
- `driverLogin` callable.

Use the correct existing authentication persistence behavior supported by the current app and browser environment.

The named Firebase app also prevents accidental sharing of the default Firebase Auth instance when testing multiple apps in the same browser origin.

Do not create a new Firebase project.

Do not use Firebase Admin SDK in frontend code.

Do not expose service-account credentials.

Do not use anonymous authentication as a substitute for Delivery authentication.

Do not authenticate using a `bizId`, `staffId`, phone number, PIN, or encoded URL value alone.

## Required login behavior

The only permitted new Delivery login path is the existing server-verified `driverLogin` Cloud Function.

Call the existing function with the same request contract.

Use the returned custom token with:

`signInWithCustomToken`

Do not implement client-side PIN verification.

Do not use bcrypt in the browser to authenticate Delivery partners.

Do not create an alternate legacy PIN fallback.

The original root app must remain untouched even if it contains old compatibility code.

## Authenticated identity

After successful sign-in:

- Listen to the Delivery app's own `onAuthStateChanged`.
- Read the authenticated Firebase ID token claims.
- Verify the expected Delivery role and relevant identity claims.
- Derive the active business, staff member, and branch from verified authentication data.
- Do not treat localStorage or URL parameters as proof of identity.
- Ensure a user cannot select another staff ID or business ID by changing the URL or browser storage.
- Ensure Delivery queries and writes use the authenticated identity and the existing Firestore permission model.

Do not silently grant business-wide access to a Delivery partner.

Do not bypass branch restrictions.

If a necessary identity claim is missing or mismatched, fail securely and show a useful error.

## Logout behavior

- Sign out from the Delivery app's Auth instance only.
- Clear Delivery-specific local/session storage values.
- Do not sign out Owner or Manager accounts.
- Do not clear unrelated application storage.
- Do not use a browser-wide storage wipe.

Do not modify the original root Firebase service.

